Abstract— Cardiovascular disease (CVD) is one of the leading causes of mortality worldwide, making early detection and timely intervention essential for improving patient outcomes. Traditional diagnostic approaches often require extensive clinical examinations and laboratory investigations, which may delay the identification of individuals at high risk. Recent advancements in Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning (ML), and the Internet of Things (IoT) have enabled the development of intelligent healthcare systems capable of supporting early disease prediction and continuous patient monitoring.

This research proposes an intelligent heart disease prediction framework that integrates multiple machine learning techniques with wearable IoT devices for real-time healthcare monitoring. Initially, a publicly available cardiovascular disease dataset is preprocessed through data cleaning, duplicate removal, feature scaling, and exploratory data analysis. Several machine learning algorithms, including Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, Gaussian Naïve Bayes, K-Nearest Neighbors, and Support Vector Machine, are evaluated to identify the most effective baseline model. The proposed work further aims to enhance prediction performance through a hybrid machine learning architecture with hyperparameter optimization. To improve clinical interpretability, Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) will be incorporated to explain the contribution of individual clinical features toward prediction outcomes. Furthermore, the proposed system is designed to integrate with wearable IoT sensors through an ESP32 microcontroller, enabling continuous physiological data acquisition and real-time risk prediction via a web-based monitoring platform.

The proposed framework seeks to improve prediction accuracy, interpretability, and practical usability in remote healthcare environments, thereby providing an efficient decision-support system for early cardiovascular disease detection.

Keywords— Heart Disease Prediction, Machine Learning, Hybrid Learning, Explainable Artificial Intelligence, SHAP, Internet of Things, ESP32, Healthcare Monitoring, Cardiovascular Disease.

1. Introduction

Cardiovascular disease (CVD) remains one of the most significant public health challenges across the world and is responsible for millions of deaths every year. According to global health reports, the increasing prevalence of sedentary lifestyles, unhealthy dietary habits, obesity, diabetes, hypertension, and aging populations has contributed significantly to the growing incidence of heart-related disorders. Early identification of individuals at risk can considerably reduce mortality through timely medical intervention and preventive healthcare.

Conventional heart disease diagnosis primarily relies on clinical examinations, laboratory investigations, electrocardiograms (ECG), imaging techniques, and physician expertise. Although these approaches provide reliable diagnoses, they often require considerable time, specialized medical infrastructure, and repeated hospital visits. Such limitations make continuous monitoring and early risk assessment difficult, particularly for patients living in remote or resource-constrained regions.

The rapid development of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) has transformed modern healthcare by enabling automated disease prediction using patient health records and clinical parameters. Various supervised machine learning algorithms such as Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, Support Vector Machine, K-Nearest Neighbors, and Naïve Bayes have demonstrated promising performance in predicting cardiovascular diseases. These techniques assist healthcare professionals by identifying complex relationships among multiple clinical features and generating predictive insights that support medical decision-making.

Despite these advancements, many existing heart disease prediction systems exhibit several limitations. Most approaches rely on a single machine learning algorithm, employ limited optimization techniques, provide minimal explanation for prediction outcomes, and remain confined to offline clinical environments. Furthermore, several systems lack integration with real-time wearable devices, thereby reducing their applicability for continuous patient monitoring.

To address these limitations, this research proposes an intelligent heart disease prediction system that combines multiple machine learning algorithms with a hybrid learning framework to improve prediction performance. The proposed methodology includes comprehensive data preprocessing, feature scaling, comparative evaluation of multiple machine learning models, hyperparameter optimization, and hybrid model development. Additionally, Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) using SHAP is incorporated to improve transparency by illustrating the contribution of individual features toward each prediction. To enhance practical deployment, the proposed framework is further designed for integration with wearable IoT devices through an ESP32-based architecture, enabling continuous physiological monitoring and real-time disease risk prediction through a web-based application.

The major contributions of this work are summarized as follows:

Development of a comprehensive machine learning pipeline for heart disease prediction using multiple supervised learning algorithms.
Design of a hybrid machine learning framework with hyperparameter optimization to improve predictive performance.
Integration of Explainable Artificial Intelligence using SHAP to enhance transparency and clinical interpretability.
Design of an IoT-enabled healthcare architecture for real-time patient monitoring using wearable sensors and ESP32.
Development of a web-based prediction platform to support remote healthcare and continuous cardiovascular risk assessment.

The proposed framework aims to provide an intelligent, interpretable, and scalable healthcare solution capable of supporting clinicians in the early detection of cardiovascular diseases while enabling future deployment in smart healthcare environments.
